We investigate properties of functions defined on the vertices of an $n$-dimensional Hamming space, specifically those that satisfy the constraints imposed by the Laplacian operator, denoted as $\Delta$. This study draws parallels with research on bandlimited functions and utilizes tools from probability theory, such as hypercontractivity, to derive key results. Some of the findings presented here were initially discussed in [Dittmann, Dittmann, Kurland, and Kurland, J. Combin. Theory, Ser. A (2020)] and extend those works. 

Our research includes a demonstration of the existence of functions that are not identically zero, satisfy the constraints imposed by $\Delta$, and have a constant value on a subset of the Hamming space, while having a value of zero on the rest of the space. This is achieved by identifying functions that are not identically zero, map to $\pm 1$ on a subset of the Hamming space, and have a value of zero on the rest of the space, which are known as partial delta-bands. 

